// A Loop's handles as other threads see them, and an operation issued there
// (see the package's header, WORKER-SIDE ISSUE).
//
// How it is built. 'lp.share()' makes the Loop's SHARED TABLE: a fixed
// directory of chunks of 256 entries that never move while the table lives
// (the records' own scheme, record.cone), one entry per handle-table cell, by
// the Handle's index. The Loop's thread publishes an entry when it registers a
// handle (its OS handle, kind and skip mode written, then its generation stored
// with release) and unpublishes it, under the entry's lock, before it closes the
// OS handle. Any thread finds an entry with a load and a generation check: no
// lock is taken to look.
//
// Each entry has a one-word LOCK, shared or exclusive, held across an
// operation's issue and the queueing of what follows it. A datagram's issue
// (RecvFrom, SendTo, RecvMany on a UDP handle) holds it SHARED ('lockShared'):
// any number of threads issue on one UDP socket at once, as the kernel lets
// them. Every other taker holds it EXCLUSIVE ('lock'): a TCP issue (its whole
// partial-send loop is one send, so two actors' bytes never interleave), a
// handle's cancel and close (aio's cancelSide and closeHandle), the controls,
// and the Loop's unpublish. An exclusive taker sets the exclusive bit first, so
// no shared holder enters after it, and then waits for those inside to finish:
// a stream of sends cannot starve a close, and a close never goes on while a
// thread is in a system call on the handle. So the orders the lock gives are
// kept: a cancel or close queued after the issue finds the operation on the
// Loop, one queued before it is ahead of it (a close marks the entry 'closing'
// under the lock, and nothing is issued on it then: the operation goes the
// Loop's way, behind the close, and is answered as today).
// The word: bit 31 is the exclusive bit (held, or wanted and waiting for the
// shared holders to go), bits 0-30 count the shared holders (a shared taker
// that finds the exclusive bit set takes its count back out and waits, so the
// count may be briefly above the true holders). It is uncontended in the common
// case (one atomic operation to take, one to let go); an acquisition that had
// to wait is counted (Shared.contended: each thread counts in a stripe of the
// table of its own, so the counting shares no cache line), spins a while and
// then yields its processor, so a holder that was preempted is not spun on for
// a whole quantum. The Loop's thread holds no other lock while it takes one.
//
// TCP ON THE WORKER PORT. A stream socket is worker-side (the entry's 'worker' is 1) when it came from a
// listener made with worker (accepted sockets: 'adopt' joins them to the worker port) or from a connect
// asked with worker (entry 'worker' 2 while it connects: Linux moves the fd from the Loop's epoll to the
// worker's when the connect succeeds, lxToWorker; Windows associates the socket with the worker port
// before connecting, and 'udpCompleted' posts the connect's completion on to the Loop's port, 'loopPort').
// Recv, RecvReady (a zero-byte receive on Windows, a MSG_PEEK on Linux) and Send (one buffer or a gather)
// on it are issued like a datagram's: 'issue' tries under the entry's lock, 'issueWaiting' lists what is
// left, the port's 'udpReady' / 'udpCompleted' answers it, and all of these (and the end of a record, an
// unpublish, a cancel) hold the entry EXCLUSIVE, since a stream's partial send and its order are one
// socket's. ORDERS. Linux: a new receive or send does not try while others wait on the socket (rwait,
// swait), so none overtakes; a send the socket took in part is kept (state stFlight, 'done' moved) and
// continues from there, never handed to the Loop. Windows: no such rule, because a listed record is held
// by the kernel and a record listed with no kernel operation would never be answered; the kernel's
// order is the order. A record is answered when its completion is taken, so a buffer is never freed
// under a pending read. Accept, connect, reset and close stay on the Loop.
//
// CONTROLS. A connected socket's half-close, peer address and keepalive are
// each one system call on its OS handle, which the entry holds. With h's entry
// locked ('sh.lock(h)', exclusive) a thread other than the Loop's may make them:
// 'sh.shutdownSend(h)', 'sh.peerAddr(h)', 'sh.setKeepAlive(h, ka)' and
// 'sh.keepAlive(h)' do what the Loop's 'shutdown', 'peerAddr', 'setKeepAlive'
// and 'keepAlive' do (the same functions of the OS handle, loop.cone), and are
// refused as those are for a handle that is no connected socket; also for an
// entry whose close has been asked (the OS handle may be closed any moment
// after the lock is let go, so nothing is done on it). The Loop unpublishes an
// entry under its lock before it closes the OS handle, so the call is never on
// a closed one. A handle with no published entry is the Loop's to do.
//
// ITS LIFE. The table outlives its Loop: a thread that holds a Shared may look
// at it (a lock, a generation check) after the Loop has died, and finds every
// entry unpublished, so nothing is issued. So the table is counted, one count
// for the Loop and one for each Shared handed out ('clone', which 'lp.share()'
// does), and the Slab, its directory and every chunk are freed by whoever lets
// go last (Shared's final). A Shared is Move, so each holder is one count, and
// a thread that only borrows one (&Shared) adds none: no count is touched to
// look, lock or issue.
//
// ISSUE. 'sh.issue(p)', with the entry of p's handle locked, tries a receive or
// a send of record p on the calling thread (Recv, and Send of one buffer or a
// gather of a chain's segments, on a socket; an empty gather is the Loop's; RecvFrom
// and SendTo, one datagram each and never partial, on a UDP handle). Windows: WSARecv/WSASend on a
// socket whose completions skip the port on success; an answer at once fills
// the record (issueDone: the Loop never sees it), a pending one leaves the
// record the kernel's (issuePending: its caller hands it to the Loop with
// lp.start, which links it, and its completion, if it came first, was kept for
// that). Linux: recv/send on the non-blocking socket; one that would block
// before anything moved is not issued (issueNot: it goes the Loop's way), a
// send that moved part of its bytes keeps them ('done') and is handed over in
// flight (issuePending). Anything else -- another kind, a deadline or a group
// already done with, a buffer with no room, a handle closing or gone -- is
// issueNot, and goes the Loop's way unchanged.
//
// STARTED UNDER THE SHARED LOCK (Windows, a datagram operation on a worker-side socket). THE RULE THIS
// REPLACES: every such operation was started under the EXCLUSIVE lock, so that one the kernel kept (pending)
// was listed in the entry before the lock was let go, with no gap in which its completion, taken by another
// worker, could find it unlisted. That held the entry across every WSASendTo, 0.83-0.89 s of every second at
// 5,000 connections, though the kernel runs concurrent sends on one socket in parallel (row 553's report,
// sections 4 and 7). NOW a send, and a receive, is started under the SHARED lock, as Linux's is,
// and only an operation the kernel keeps takes the exclusive lock, to be listed. (A receive goes pending
// whenever no datagram is waiting, so the gap below is met all the time, and the ordinary suites run through
// it; and the receives' exclusive takes no longer stall every sender.) The lock has no upgrade (an exclusive
// taker waits for the shared holders to go, so an issuer holding the shared bit cannot take the exclusive bit
// without letting go of it first), so on pending there is a GAP between the kernel call and the listing, in
// which the completion can be taken by another worker, a cancel or a deadline can find nothing listed
// (endWaiting), and the entry can be closed (unpublished: no thread can take its lock then, ever). The lock
// cannot say who goes first; THE RECORD DECIDES, by one word, 'ustate':
//   1. The issuer, under the shared lock, BEFORE the kernel call, stores usStarted (the call's own packet is
//      what the completion is, so whoever takes the completion has the store behind it, as it has the
//      kernel's status in 'ov.internal'). A call that answers at once, or fails, puts usNone back: no packet
//      will come.
//   2. A completion ('udpCompleted') first stores its bytes and stamp in the record, then compare-and-swaps
//      usStarted to usCompleted. If it WINS, the record is the issuer's to answer: the completion neither
//      lists nor answers nor pushes it, and touches it no more. If it LOSES (the issuer got there first:
//      usListed or usLeft, or the record was not started this way), it goes on as ever: it takes the entry,
//      unlists the record, answers it.
//   3. The issuer, on pending, answers issueStarted. Its caller lets go of the shared lock, counts the wait,
//      and calls 'listStarted(p)', which takes the entry EXCLUSIVE if it still can. Under it, it
//      compare-and-swaps usStarted to usListed. If it WINS it lists the record, then looks again at the
//      deadline and the group (a cancel or a deadline reached in the gap never un-happens, and endWaiting,
//      which finds an unlisted record waiting for nothing, answered false to it: so the abort is asked here,
//      as endWaiting would have), lets go, and answers issuePending: the record is the table's, and its
//      completion answers it. If it LOSES (usCompleted), the completion came first and the record is answered
//      here from what it stored: issueDone, its bytes and its status as the completion would have answered
//      them. If the entry cannot be taken (the handle was closed in the gap), it compare-and-swaps usStarted
//      to usLeft: it WINS, and the abort the close made (closing a socket aborts what the kernel holds on
//      it) is a completion that answers the record as ever (no entry, so nothing to unlist), or LOSES
//      (usCompleted) and answers it here.
//   WHY IT HOLDS. Both parties compare-and-swap the one word from the one value, usStarted, so exactly one
//   wins: the winner of a listing or a leaving hands the answer to the completion, the winner of a completion
//   hands it to the issuer, and the loser sees which and acts, so the record is answered once (not twice,
//   not never). Neither party frees or answers a record after its own swap except as the swap says, and the
//   record's owner (the kernel, until its completion is taken, and then whoever the word names) lets it go
//   only after both have swapped, so nothing is freed under the other. A completion that LOST the swap to a
//   listing waits for the entry's lock, which 'listStarted' holds from its swap until the record is listed,
//   so the completion never unlists a record before it is on the list. Nothing is ever listed that has
//   been answered: the listing happens only on the winning swap, under the lock.

// What 'issue' did
pub const issueNot = 0u32;       // nothing: the record goes the Loop's way, as if never tried
pub const issueDone = 1u32;      // answered here: the record is the caller's, its outcome in it
pub const issuePending = 2u32;   // in flight: the caller hands it to the Loop (lp.start)
pub const issueStarted = 3u32;   // Windows, a worker-side datagram started under the SHARED lock, and pending: the kernel holds
                                 // it, not yet listed; the caller lets go of the lock and calls 'listStarted' (below)

// A record's 'ustate' (STARTED UNDER THE SHARED LOCK): who answers it, decided by compare-and-swap on the record
const usNone = 0u32;         // not started that way (every record's state but for the window below)
const usStarted = 1u32;      // set by the issuer under the shared lock, before the kernel call: started, not listed
const usListed = 2u32;       // the issuer listed it: its completion goes on to unlist it and answer it
const usCompleted = 3u32;    // its completion came first: bytes and status stored, the issuer answers it
const usLeft = 4u32;         // the issuer could not take the entry (closed): its completion answers it, as ever

const tryPublic = 0u32;      // issueAt's modes
const tryWaiting = 1u32;
const tryRetry = 2u32;

const shChunk = 256u32;          // entries a chunk
const shChunks = 4096u32;        // chunks at most: 1,048,576 handles
const shSpins = 64u32;           // looks at a held lock before each yield
const exBit = 0x80000000u32;     // the lock word's exclusive bit: held, or wanted
const shMask = 0x7FFFFFFFu32;    // its shared holders
const stripes = 64u32;           // the contended count's stripes (8 words, a cache line, each)

// One handle's entry: 64 bytes, so two handles' locks are not one cache line
struct SEntry {
  gen: u32;                // the Handle's generation while published; 0 while not (atomic)
  lock: u32;               // the lock word: exclusive bit, shared holders (atomic)
  closing: u32;            // its close has been asked: nothing is issued on it (under the lock)
  kind: u32;               // hSocket, ...
  skip: u32;               // Windows: its completions skip the port on success
  worker: u32;             // a UDP socket whose waits are the worker port's, not the Loop's (UDP ON THE WORKER PORT)
  hold: u32;               // tests: datagram sends are answered as if the socket would block (holdSends)
  spare: u32;
  raw: usize;              // the OS handle
  rwait: *OpCore;          // under the EXCLUSIVE lock: the receives waiting worker-side, oldest first (hnext, hprev)
  swait: *OpCore;          // likewise the sends: Linux, those the socket would not take, tried in order at an
                           // EPOLLOUT edge; Windows, those the kernel holds
  rcancels: u32;           // Windows: how many times the handle's reads were asked to end (askWaiting), under the EXCLUSIVE lock
  wcancels: u32;           // likewise the writes. A datagram started under the shared lock notes them, and looks again when listed
}

// The table: the chunk directory, the contended count, and how many hold it
struct Slab {
  dir: *usize;
  cont: *u64;              // the contended count: 'stripes' stripes of 8 words (atomic)
  holders: u64;            // the Loop and every Shared handed out (atomic)
  loopPort: usize;         // Windows: the Loop's completion port, where a worker posts a connect's completion on (TCP ON THE WORKER PORT)
}

// How many tables are made and not yet freed: for tests, and for finding leaks
let sharedLive: Atomic[u32];

// Which stripe of a table's contended count a thread counts in: handed out in
// turn at its first count (stripeAt is that stripe plus one; 0 until then)
let stripeNext: Atomic[u32];
var @threadlocal stripeAt = 0u32;

pub fn sharedTables() u32 {sharedLive.load()}

// A Loop's shared table, as any thread holds it: Sendable, one holder each, a
// 'clone' for another (WORKER-SIDE ISSUE). The last holder to let go frees it
pub struct Shared is Move, Sendable {
  at: usize;               // the Slab, or 0: none

  // No table: nothing is ever issued through it
  pub fn none() Shared {Shared(0usize)}

  pub fn isNone(self: &) bool {at == 0usize}

  // Another holder of the same table
  pub fn clone(self: &) Shared {
    if at != 0usize {
      mem.atomicAdd[u64](&(*(at as *Slab)).holders as *u64, 1u64, MemOrder.Relaxed);
    }
    Shared(at)
  }

  // This holder lets go; the last frees the table. The Loop's thread wrote
  // every chunk: the count's release and acquire order those writes before the
  // free, whichever holder is last
  fn final(self: &uni) {
    if at == 0usize {return;}
    let s = at as *Slab;
    if mem.atomicSub[u64](&(*s).holders as *u64, 1u64, MemOrder.Release) != 1u64 {return;}
    mem.atomicLoad[u64](&(*s).holders as *u64, MemOrder.Acquire);
    var ci = 0u32;
    while ci < shChunks {
      libc.free(mem.atomicLoad[usize]((*s).dir + usize.from(ci), MemOrder.Relaxed) as *u8);
      ci = ci + 1u32;
    }
    libc.free((*s).dir as *u8);
    libc.free((*s).cont as *u8);
    libc.free(s as *u8);
    sharedLive.sub(1u32);
  }

  // The lock acquisitions that had to wait for the lock, since the table was
  // made: the sum of every thread's stripe
  pub fn contended(self: &) u64 {
    if at == 0usize {return 0u64;}
    let c = (*(at as *Slab)).cont;
    var sum = 0u64;
    var k = 0u32;
    while k < stripes {
      sum = sum + mem.atomicLoad[u64](c + usize.from(k * 8u32), MemOrder.Relaxed);
      k = k + 1u32;
    }
    sum
  }

  // One more acquisition that had to wait, counted in the calling thread's stripe
  fn countWait(self: &) {
    if stripeAt == 0u32 {stripeAt = stripeNext.add(1u32) % stripes + 1u32;}
    let p = (*(at as *Slab)).cont + usize.from((stripeAt - 1u32) * 8u32);
    mem.atomicStore[u64](p, mem.atomicLoad[u64](p, MemOrder.Relaxed) + 1u64, MemOrder.Relaxed);
  }

  // The entry for cell i, or null when no chunk holds it yet
  fn entry(self: &, i: u32) *SEntry {
    let ci = i / shChunk;
    if at == 0usize || ci >= shChunks {return null;}
    let c = mem.atomicLoad[usize]((*(at as *Slab)).dir + usize.from(ci), MemOrder.Acquire);
    if c == 0usize {return null;}
    (c as *SEntry) + usize.from(i % shChunk)
  }

  // h's entry locked EXCLUSIVE, from any thread: true if h is published (its
  // entry then held until unlock); false, and nothing held, if it names
  // nothing here
  pub fn lock(self: &, h: Handle) bool {
    if at == 0usize || h.isNone() {return false;}
    let e = self.entry(h.index);
    if e as usize == 0usize {return false;}
    if mem.atomicLoad[u32](&(*e).gen as *u32, MemOrder.Acquire) != h.gen {return false;}
    self.acquire(e);
    if mem.atomicLoad[u32](&(*e).gen as *u32, MemOrder.Acquire) != h.gen {
      self.release(e);
      return false;
    }
    true
  }

  // h's entry let go: h was locked by this thread
  pub fn unlock(self: &, h: Handle) {
    self.release(self.entry(h.index));
  }

  // h's entry locked SHARED, for a datagram's issue (RecvFrom, SendTo or
  // RecvMany on a UDP handle): it reads the entry and makes one system call on
  // its OS handle, and any number of threads may do so at once; no exclusive
  // taker is in while one is. As 'lock' otherwise: false, and nothing held, if
  // h names nothing here
  pub fn lockShared(self: &, h: Handle) bool {
    if at == 0usize || h.isNone() {return false;}
    let e = self.entry(h.index);
    if e as usize == 0usize {return false;}
    if mem.atomicLoad[u32](&(*e).gen as *u32, MemOrder.Acquire) != h.gen {return false;}
    self.acquireShared(e);
    if mem.atomicLoad[u32](&(*e).gen as *u32, MemOrder.Acquire) != h.gen {
      self.releaseShared(e);
      return false;
    }
    true
  }

  // h's entry let go: h was locked shared by this thread
  pub fn unlockShared(self: &, h: Handle) {
    self.releaseShared(self.entry(h.index));
  }

  // h's close has been asked: nothing is issued on it from now on. h is
  // locked by this thread
  pub fn markClosing(self: &, h: Handle) {
    (*self.entry(h.index)).closing = 1u32;
  }

  // The OS handle of h's entry, which is a connected socket not closing; else
  // invalidHandle. h is locked by this thread (CONTROLS)
  fn socketRaw(self: &, h: Handle) usize {
    let e = self.entry(h.index);
    if e as usize == 0usize || (*e).closing != 0u32 || (*e).kind != hSocket {return invalidHandle;}
    (*e).raw
  }

  // h's sending side finished (Loop.shutdown's work): 0, or the system's
  // number; noSuchHandle() for an entry closing or not a connected socket.
  // h is locked by this thread (CONTROLS)
  pub fn shutdownSend(self: &, h: Handle) u32 {
    let raw = self.socketRaw(h);
    if raw == invalidHandle {return noSuchHandle();}
    rawShutdownSend(raw)
  }

  // h's peer (Loop.peerAddr's work); as shutdownSend for a handle that is not
  // a usable connected socket
  pub fn peerAddr(self: &, h: Handle) Result[SocketAddr, IoError] {
    let raw = self.socketRaw(h);
    if raw == invalidHandle {return Err[SocketAddr, IoError](IoError(noSuchHandle()));}
    rawPeerAddr(raw)
  }

  // TCP keepalive set on h (Loop.setKeepAlive's work): 0, or the system's
  // number; as shutdownSend for a handle that is not a usable connected socket
  pub fn setKeepAlive(self: &, h: Handle, ka: KeepAlive) u32 {
    let raw = self.socketRaw(h);
    if raw == invalidHandle {return noSuchHandle();}
    rawSetKeepAlive(raw, ka)
  }

  // h's TCP keepalive as the system reads it back (Loop.keepAlive's work);
  // as shutdownSend for a handle that is not a usable connected socket
  pub fn keepAlive(self: &, h: Handle) Result[KeepAlive, IoError] {
    let raw = self.socketRaw(h);
    if raw == invalidHandle {return Err[KeepAlive, IoError](IoError(noSuchHandle()));}
    rawKeepAlive(raw)
  }

  // Exclusive: claim the exclusive bit (no shared taker enters after it), then
  // wait for the shared holders already in to let go: one system call's wait.
  // The bit is claimed by a compare-and-swap, so two exclusive takers are
  // never both in
  fn acquire(self: &, e: *SEntry) {
    let lk = &(*e).lock as *u32;
    var waited = false;
    var spins = 0u32;
    while true {
      let v = mem.atomicLoad[u32](lk, MemOrder.Relaxed);
      if v & exBit == 0u32 {
        let old, ok = mem.atomicCompareSwap[u32](lk, v, v | exBit, MemOrder.Acquire, MemOrder.Relaxed);
        if ok {break;}
      } else {
        if !waited {
          waited = true;
          self.countWait();
        }
        spins = spins + 1u32;
        if spins >= shSpins {
          spins = 0u32;
          thread.yieldNow();
        }
      }
    }
    // The bit is ours. A shared taker that finds it set takes its count back
    // out, so the count falls to nothing
    spins = 0u32;
    while mem.atomicLoad[u32](lk, MemOrder.Acquire) & shMask != 0u32 {
      if !waited {
        waited = true;
        self.countWait();
      }
      spins = spins + 1u32;
      if spins >= shSpins {
        spins = 0u32;
        thread.yieldNow();
      }
    }
  }

  // Not a store: a shared taker may be backing its count out as this goes
  fn release(self: &, e: *SEntry) {
    mem.atomicSub[u32](&(*e).lock as *u32, exBit, MemOrder.Release);
  }

  // Shared: take a count at once; if the exclusive bit was set, take the count
  // back out, wait for the bit to clear, and try again
  fn acquireShared(self: &, e: *SEntry) {
    let lk = &(*e).lock as *u32;
    var waited = false;
    var spins = 0u32;
    while true {
      if mem.atomicAdd[u32](lk, 1u32, MemOrder.Acquire) & exBit == 0u32 {return;}
      mem.atomicSub[u32](lk, 1u32, MemOrder.Release);
      if !waited {
        waited = true;
        self.countWait();
      }
      while mem.atomicLoad[u32](lk, MemOrder.Relaxed) & exBit != 0u32 {
        spins = spins + 1u32;
        if spins >= shSpins {
          spins = 0u32;
          thread.yieldNow();
        }
      }
    }
  }

  fn releaseShared(self: &, e: *SEntry) {
    mem.atomicSub[u32](&(*e).lock as *u32, 1u32, MemOrder.Release);
  }

  // Try record p's receive or send here (ISSUE above). The entry of p's
  // handle is locked by this thread. issueNot, issueDone, issuePending or
  // issueStarted. It never answers issuePending for a worker-side socket (UDP
  // ON THE WORKER PORT): a datagram is never partial, so on Linux it is answered
  // or issueNot; on Windows a datagram operation is answered, or issueStarted
  // (the kernel holds it: STARTED UNDER THE SHARED LOCK, whose caller then calls
  // 'listStarted' with no lock held); 'issueWaiting' lists what 'issue' leaves
  // (issueNot), and so answers issuePending itself
  pub fn issue(self: &, p: *OpCore) u32 {self.issueAt(p, tryPublic)}

  // 'issue' in one of three modes, which differ for a worker-side socket
  // (UDP ON THE WORKER PORT). A datagram send waits behind the sends already
  // waiting on it (Linux: the socket's EPOLLOUT edge tries them in order, and
  // a send tried past them would overtake): every mode but the edge's own
  // retry of the head, tryRetry. A datagram operation on Windows, a receive as
  // much as a send, is started under the entry's shared lock by tryPublic, which
  // is 'issue', and one the kernel keeps answers issueStarted, to be listed by
  // 'listStarted' (STARTED UNDER THE SHARED LOCK); issueWaiting's start under the
  // exclusive lock, tryWaiting, is for what 'issue' declined (a handle closing,
  // a deadline reached, a socket whose completions do not skip the port)
  fn issueAt(self: &, p: *OpCore, mode: u32) u32 {
    let r = p as &mut OpCore;
    let e = self.entry(r.handle.index);
    if e as usize == 0usize || (*e).closing != 0u32 {return issueNot;}
    let k = r.kind;
    let hk = (*e).kind;
    let tcp = hk == hSocket && (k == diag.kindRecv || k == diag.kindSend || k == diag.kindRecvReady);
    let udp = hk == hDatagram && (k == diag.kindRecvFrom || k == diag.kindSendTo || k == diag.kindRecvMany);
    if !tcp && !udp {return issueNot;}
    // An empty gather is the Loop's (its zero-length send answers Done(0)); on a worker-side
    // socket issueWaiting answers it
    if k == diag.kindSend && r.isGather() && r.gatherSegments() == 0usize {return issueNot;}
    if (k == diag.kindRecv || k == diag.kindRecvFrom || k == diag.kindRecvMany) && r.bufRoom() == 0usize {return issueNot;}
    #if #os == .windows { if (*e).skip == 0u32 {return issueNot;} }
    // TCP ON THE WORKER PORT: a receive or send on a worker-side stream waits behind the ones already
    // waiting on it (a read must not take bytes ahead of an earlier read, nor a send write between an
    // earlier send's): every mode but the edge's own retry of the head. On Windows its kernel operation
    // may go pending, which must be listed with no gap in which its completion could come first, so it
    // is started under the exclusive lock by issueWaiting alone (tryWaiting)
    let tcpWorker = tcp && (*e).worker != 0u32;
    if tcpWorker {
      // (Windows: the kernel orders overlapped operations on a socket as they were started, and every
      // one listed there is the kernel's, so none waits unstarted behind another)
      #if #os == .windows {
        if mode == tryPublic {return issueNot;}
      } #else {
        if mode != tryRetry && (if k == diag.kindSend {(*e).swait} else {(*e).rwait}) as usize != 0usize {return issueNot;}
      }
    }
    // A send on a worker-side socket that is held, or has sends waiting ahead of it, is not tried. The
    // entry's wait lists change only under the exclusive lock, which no shared holder shares. (Windows:
    // no such rule, the kernel orders what it holds; a datagram operation, a receive as much as a send, is
    // started under the shared lock, STARTED UNDER THE SHARED LOCK)
    #if #os != .windows {
      if udp && (*e).worker != 0u32 && k == diag.kindSendTo {
        if (*e).hold != 0u32 {return issueNot;}
        if mode != tryRetry && (*e).swait as usize != 0usize {return issueNot;}
      }
    }
    // A deadline or a group done with is answered by the Loop, as at a start
    let now = Instant.now();
    if dueOutcome(r, now) != 0u32 {return issueNot;}
    #if #os == .windows {
      // A datagram on a worker-side socket tried under the shared lock (tryPublic) is started there, and
      // left for 'listStarted' if the kernel keeps it
      let started = udp && (*e).worker != 0u32 && mode == tryPublic;
      if started {noteCancels(e, r);}
      winIssue(p, (*e).raw, now, (*e).worker != 0u32, started)
    } #else {
      let got = lxIssue(p, (*e).raw, now, (*e).worker != 0u32);
      // A send that moved part of its bytes on a worker-side stream stays here: it goes on from 'done'
      // under issueWaiting, which lists it, never again from the start and never on the Loop
      if got == issuePending && tcpWorker && mode == tryPublic {return issueNot;}
      got
    }
  }

  // UDP ON THE WORKER PORT. A datagram operation on a socket bound with the
  // Loop's worker port switched on (lp.setWorkerPort) never waits on the
  // Loop: the Loop answers it Failed, invalid handle. A try that finds nothing
  // to do (a receive with no datagram, a send the socket will not take) is
  // given to 'issueWaiting', with no lock held. It takes the entry EXCLUSIVE
  // (the wait lists change only then), tries once more (an edge that came
  // between is not missed), and lists the record: issuePending, the record is
  // the table's, and one of 'udpReady' (Linux: readiness), 'udpCompleted'
  // (Windows: the kernel's completion) or 'endWaiting' answers it, once, into
  // the list its caller gives. Anything it can answer at once is answered
  // there (issueDone: a handle that names nothing or is closing, no room for
  // a receive, a group cancelled, a deadline past, or the second try's own
  // datagram). issueNot: not a datagram operation, or no worker-side socket:
  // the Loop's way. The caller holds neither lock
  pub fn issueWaiting(self: &, p: *OpCore) u32 {
    let r = p as &mut OpCore;
    let k = r.kind;
    let dgram = k == diag.kindRecvFrom || k == diag.kindRecvMany || k == diag.kindSendTo;
    let stream = k == diag.kindRecv || k == diag.kindRecvReady || k == diag.kindSend;
    if !dgram && !stream {return issueNot;}
    let h = r.handle;
    let now = Instant.now();
    if !self.lock(h) {
      stampIssued(p, now);
      return answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeFailed, 0usize, noSuchHandle());
    }
    let e = self.entry(h.index);
    if (*e).worker == 0u32 || (*e).kind != (if dgram {hDatagram} else {hSocket}) {
      self.unlock(h);
      return issueNot;
    }
    var got = issueNot;
    let due = dueOutcome(r, now);
    if (*e).closing != 0u32 {
      stampIssued(p, now);
      got = answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeFailed, 0usize, noSuchHandle());
    } else if (k == diag.kindRecv || k == diag.kindRecvFrom || k == diag.kindRecvMany) && r.bufRoom() == 0usize {
      stampIssued(p, now);
      got = answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeFailed, 0usize, noRoom());
    } else if k == diag.kindSend && r.isGather() && r.gatherSegments() == 0usize {
      // An empty gather: nothing to send, Done(0), as the Loop answers it
      stampIssued(p, now);
      got = answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeDone, 0usize, 0u32);
    } else if due != 0u32 {
      stampIssued(p, now);
      got = answeredHere(p, due, 0usize, 0u32);
    } else {
      got = self.issueAt(p, tryWaiting);
      // The try looks at the deadline and the group itself, a moment after the look above, and a deadline
      // reached between the two makes it decline with nothing started. Listing the record then would leave
      // a record no kernel operation holds: on Windows nothing completes it, and the group's end (endWaiting)
      // asks the kernel to abort an operation that is not there, so the read would never be answered, its
      // deadline lost with it. A deadline reached, or a group cancelled, never un-happens, so what the try
      // declined for it is due here too, and is answered at once, as above
      var late = 0u32;
      if got == issueNot {late = dueOutcome(r, Instant.now());}
      if late != 0u32 {
        stampIssued(p, now);
        got = answeredHere(p, late, 0usize, 0u32);
      } else if got == issueNot {
        // Nothing to take, or a send held: not stamped yet (the try stamps what it answers). A send
        // that moved part of its bytes is stamped already, its 'done' kept (TCP ON THE WORKER PORT)
        if r.state != stFlight {stampIssued(p, now);}
        listWait(e, p);
        got = issuePending;
      } else if got == issuePending {
        // Windows: the kernel holds it, and its completion comes to the worker port, never to the
        // Loop. Its completion's answer waits for this entry's lock, which is held until it is listed
        listWait(e, p);
      }
    }
    self.unlock(h);
    got
  }

  // For tests, Linux only (a no-op on Windows): while 'on', every datagram
  // send on h's worker-side socket answers as a send the socket would not take
  // (EAGAIN), so a send can be held waiting on a loopback socket that never
  // would. The sends it held go on at the next 'udpReady' with the EPOLLOUT bit
  pub fn holdSends(self: &, h: Handle, on: bool) {
    #if #os == .windows {return;} #else {
    if !self.lock(h) {return;}
    (*self.entry(h.index)).hold = if on {1u32} else {0u32};
    self.unlock(h);
    }
  }

  // Linux: the worker port's epoll reported 'events' for the entry and
  // generation its datum held. The receives waiting on it try again, in order
  // while they take a datagram, and so do the sends, in order while the
  // socket takes them (readiness is edge-triggered: each list is tried until
  // it would block). Every record answered is appended to out, to be answered
  // on by the caller; the others stay listed. A stale datum (the handle is
  // gone) does nothing. Any thread; takes the entry exclusive. A no-op on Windows
  pub fn udpReady(self: &, index: u32, gen: u32, events: u32, out: &mut List[*OpCore]) {
    #if #os == .windows {return;} #else {
    let h = Handle(index, gen);
    if !self.lock(h) {return;}
    let e = self.entry(index);
    if (*e).worker != 0u32 && (*e).closing == 0u32 {
      if (events & (epollIn | epollErr | epollHup | epollRdHup)) != 0u32 {self.drainWaits(e, true, out);}
      if (events & (epollOut | epollErr | epollHup)) != 0u32 {self.drainWaits(e, false, out);}
    }
    self.unlock(h);
    }
  }

  // Entry e's receives (or sends) tried in order, until one would block; e
  // held exclusive (Linux)
  fn drainWaits(self: &, e: *SEntry, reads: bool, out: &mut List[*OpCore]) {
    while true {
      let p = if reads {(*e).rwait} else {(*e).swait};
      if p as usize == 0usize {break;}
      let r = p as &mut OpCore;
      let sub = r.submitted;
      let now = Instant.now();
      let due = dueOutcome(r, now);
      var got = issueDone;
      if due != 0u32 {
        // Its group was cancelled or its deadline passed while it waited
        answeredHere(p, due, 0usize, 0u32);
      } else {
        got = self.issueAt(p, tryRetry);
      }
      if got != issueDone {break;}
      // Answered by this try: its first stamp is its submit time
      r.submitted = sub;
      unlistWait(e, p);
      out.push(p);
    }
  }

  // Windows: the worker port's completion for record p (its OVERLAPPED is the
  // record, the port's key is the socket's), with the bytes it moved. The
  // record leaves its entry's list (a handle already closed has none) and is
  // answered, as the Loop answers a completion: a deadline's or a cancel's
  // abort completion is TimedOut or Cancelled, the record whole with its
  // buffer and its batch; a completion that came first is Done. Appended to
  // out. A record started under the shared lock and not yet listed
  // (STARTED UNDER THE SHARED LOCK) is not answered here when this completion
  // wins its word: 'listStarted' answers it. A no-op on Linux
  pub fn udpCompleted(self: &, p: *OpCore, bytes: u32, out: &mut List[*OpCore]) {
    #if #os == .windows {
    let r = p as &mut OpCore;
    let h = r.handle;
    // A worker-side connect (TCP ON THE WORKER PORT): its socket joined the worker port before it was
    // connected; the Loop made it and finishes it, so the completion goes on to the Loop's own port
    // as it is (the kernel's status is in the record's OVERLAPPED)
    if r.kind == diag.kindConnect {
      PostQueuedCompletionStatus((*(at as *Slab)).loopPort, bytes, keyIo, p as *u8);
      return;
    }
    r.dequeued = Instant.now();
    // Started and not yet listed: the bytes are stored, and the issuer answers it (the swap decides who)
    r.stashBytes = bytes;
    if swapState(r, usStarted, usCompleted) {return;}
    if self.lock(h) {
      unlistWait(self.entry(h.index), p);
      self.unlock(h);
    }
    self.finishCompleted(p, bytes);
    out.push(p);
    } #else {}
  }

  // Windows: record p, started under the shared lock and left pending (issueStarted), listed or answered, by its
  // issuer, with no lock held (STARTED UNDER THE SHARED LOCK). issuePending: the record is the table's, and
  // its completion answers it, exactly once (a cancel, or a deadline, reached in the gap is asked here); or
  // issueDone: its completion came first, and the record is answered here, from what it stored, as that
  // completion would have answered it (the caller answers it on, as for issueWaiting's issueDone); or
  // issueNot: it was not started so. A no-op on Linux (issueNot)
  pub fn listStarted(self: &, p: *OpCore) u32 {
    #if #os == .windows {
    let r = p as &mut OpCore;
    let h = r.handle;
    if self.lock(h) {
      let e = self.entry(h.index);
      if swapState(r, usStarted, usListed) {
        listWait(e, p);
        // A cancel or a deadline reached in the gap never un-happens, and endWaiting, which found nothing
        // listed, answered false to it: the abort is asked here, as endWaiting asks it
        var due = dueOutcome(r, Instant.now());
        // A handle-level cancel (askWaiting: cancelRead, cancelWrite) in the gap found the record unlisted, and
        // the socket stays open, so nothing else would end it: the entry's count of cancels moved since the start
        let now = if r.kind == diag.kindSendTo || r.kind == diag.kindSend {(*e).wcancels} else {(*e).rcancels};
        if due == 0u32 && now != r.cepoch {due = diag.outcomeCancelled;}
        if due != 0u32 {
          if r.asked == askedNone {r.asked = if due == diag.outcomeTimedOut {askedDeadline} else {askedCancel};}
          CancelIoEx((*e).raw, p as *u8);
        }
        self.unlock(h);
        return issuePending;
      }
      self.unlock(h);
    } else if swapState(r, usStarted, usLeft) {
      // Closed in the gap: the abort the close made is a completion, and answers it as one that was not listed
      return issuePending;
    }
    if mem.atomicLoad[u32](&r.ustate as *u32, MemOrder.Acquire) != usCompleted {return issueNot;}
    self.finishCompleted(p, r.stashBytes);
    issueDone
    } #else {issueNot}
  }

  // For tests (Windows; issueNot on Linux): the start of a datagram operation under the SHARED lock, which the caller
  // holds, as 'issue' makes it for a send, whatever its kind: a receive with nothing to take answers issueStarted
  // here, which loopback never does for a send. The caller then does what other threads do in the gap, and
  // calls 'listStarted' (iocore's startedsend test)
  pub fn startedForTest(self: &, p: *OpCore) u32 {
    #if #os == .windows {
    let r = p as &mut OpCore;
    let e = self.entry(r.handle.index);
    if e as usize == 0usize || (*e).worker == 0u32 || (*e).skip == 0u32 {return issueNot;}
    noteCancels(e, r);
    winIssue(p, (*e).raw, Instant.now(), true, true)
    } #else {issueNot}
  }

  // Record p, whose completion has been taken (bytes moved; its status in its OVERLAPPED), answered as the Loop
  // answers a completion. Windows only
  fn finishCompleted(self: &, p: *OpCore, bytes: u32) {
    #if #os == .windows {
    let r = p as &mut OpCore;
    let status = r.ov.internal & 0xFFFFFFFFusize;
    let n = usize.from(bytes);
    if r.kind == diag.kindRecvMany && (status == statusSuccess || status == statusBufferOverflow) {
      r.bufGrow(n);
      r.batchOne(status == statusBufferOverflow);
      answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeDone, 1usize, 0u32);
    } else if status == statusBufferOverflow && r.kind == diag.kindRecvFrom {
      r.bufGrow(n);
      answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeFailed, 0usize, errorMsgSize);
    } else if status == statusSuccess {
      if r.kind == diag.kindRecvFrom || r.kind == diag.kindRecv {r.bufGrow(n);}
      answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeDone, n, 0u32);
    } else if status == statusEndOfFile && (r.kind == diag.kindRecv || r.kind == diag.kindRecvReady) {
      // The end of the stream, as the Loop's finish answers it
      answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeDone, 0usize, 0u32);
    } else if status == statusCancelled {
      answeredHere(p, if r.asked == askedDeadline {diag.outcomeTimedOut} else {diag.outcomeCancelled}, 0usize, 0u32);
    } else if status == statusInvalidBufferSize && r.kind == diag.kindSendTo {
      answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeFailed, 0usize, errorMsgSize);
    } else {
      answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeFailed, 0usize, RtlNtStatusToDosError(u32.from(status)));
    }
    } #else {}
  }

  // End record p, which waits on h's worker-side socket and has the id 'id'
  // (its core's), because its deadline came (why = diag.outcomeTimedOut, or its
  // group ended by its deadline) or it was cancelled (anything else): false if it is no longer waiting there,
  // answered or its handle gone. Linux: it leaves the list and is answered at
  // once, with its buffer and batch, appended to out. Windows: the kernel is
  // asked to abort it (CancelIoEx on this operation alone) and true is
  // answered; the abort's completion comes to the worker port, and
  // 'udpCompleted' answers it then, so the batch always comes back (a
  // completion that beat the abort is Done). Any thread; takes the entry exclusive
  pub fn endWaiting(self: &, h: Handle, p: *OpCore, id: OpId, why: u32, out: &mut List[*OpCore]) bool {
    if !self.lock(h) {return false;}
    let e = self.entry(h.index);
    let r = p as &mut OpCore;
    let waiting = inFlightAt(p, id.gen) && r.side != onNone && r.handle == h;
    if !waiting {
      self.unlock(h);
      return false;
    }
    // A group that ended by its deadline ends its operations TimedOut, as the Loop's own timers do
    var byDeadline = why == diag.outcomeTimedOut;
    match r.group {
      case let Some(g) {if timedOut(&g) {byDeadline = true;}}
      else {}
    }
    let outcome = if byDeadline {diag.outcomeTimedOut} else {diag.outcomeCancelled};
    #if #os == .windows {
      if r.asked == askedNone {r.asked = if outcome == diag.outcomeTimedOut {askedDeadline} else {askedCancel};}
      CancelIoEx((*e).raw, p as *u8);
    } #else {
      unlistWait(e, p);
      answeredHere(p, outcome, 0usize, 0u32);
      out.push(p);
    }
    self.unlock(h);
    true
  }
}

// What a record's group and deadline say at 'now': diag.outcomeCancelled for a
// group cancelled, diag.outcomeTimedOut for a deadline (its own or its
// group's) reached, else 0
fn dueOutcome(r: &mut OpCore, now: Instant) u32 {
  var dl = r.deadline;
  var cancelled = false;
  match r.group {
    case let Some(s) {
      if s.deadline() < dl {dl = s.deadline();}
      // A group that ended by its deadline is not a cancel: the operation answers TimedOut, as the Loop's
      // own timers answer it (timedOut, iocore.cone)
      cancelled = s.isCancelled() && !timedOut(&s);
    }
    else {}
  }
  if cancelled {return diag.outcomeCancelled;}
  if dl <= now {return diag.outcomeTimedOut;}
  0u32
}

// Record r, about to be started under the shared lock on entry e: how many cancels of its direction the entry
// has had so far (STARTED UNDER THE SHARED LOCK, a handle-level cancel in the gap)
fn noteCancels(e: *SEntry, r: &mut OpCore) {
  r.cepoch = if r.kind == diag.kindSendTo || r.kind == diag.kindSend {(*e).wcancels} else {(*e).rcancels};
}

// Record r's 'ustate' compare-and-swapped from one value to another: true if this call made the change
// (STARTED UNDER THE SHARED LOCK: the one place two threads decide who answers a record)
fn swapState(r: &mut OpCore, from: u32, to: u32) bool {
  let old, ok = mem.atomicCompareSwap[u32](&r.ustate as *u32, from, to, MemOrder.AcqRel, MemOrder.Acquire);
  ok
}

// Record p listed last among entry e's waiting receives, or sends; e is held
// exclusive. The record's own links serve, as a Loop's handle lists do. The list
// is CIRCULAR backwards: the first record's 'hprev' is the last, so the last is
// found, and a record appended, in constant time (thousands of sends can wait
// on one entry when the send buffer is full); the last's 'hnext' is null, and
// every other record's 'hprev' is the record before it
fn listWait(e: *SEntry, p: *OpCore) {
  let r = p as &mut OpCore;
  let reads = r.kind != diag.kindSendTo && r.kind != diag.kindSend;
  r.side = if reads {onReads} else {onWrites};
  r.hnext = null;
  let first = if reads {(*e).rwait} else {(*e).swait};
  if first as usize == 0usize {
    r.hprev = p;
    if reads {(*e).rwait = p;} else {(*e).swait = p;}
    return;
  }
  let last = (*first).hprev;
  (*last).hnext = p;
  r.hprev = last;
  (*first).hprev = p;
}

// Record p taken off the list of entry e it is on, if it is on one; e is held
// exclusive (the list is circular backwards: see listWait)
fn unlistWait(e: *SEntry, p: *OpCore) {
  let r = p as &mut OpCore;
  if r.side == onNone {return;}
  let prev = r.hprev;
  let next = r.hnext;
  let reads = r.side == onReads;
  let first = if reads {(*e).rwait} else {(*e).swait};
  if first as usize == p as usize {
    // The first: the next becomes first, and its 'hprev' is the last, which the first's was
    if reads {(*e).rwait = next;} else {(*e).swait = next;}
    if next as usize != 0usize {(*next).hprev = prev;}
  } else {
    (*prev).hnext = next;
    if next as usize != 0usize {(*next).hprev = prev;} else {(*first).hprev = prev;}
  }
  r.side = onNone;
  r.hprev = null;
  r.hnext = null;
}

// Entry h's waiting receives (or sends) asked to end, for the Loop's cancelHandle (UDP ON THE WORKER
// PORT): Linux, they are taken off the entry's list under its exclusive lock, and the chain (linked by
// hnext, the side marks cleared) is returned for the Loop to answer Cancelled, as 'unpublish' answers
// what waits on a closed handle; Windows, the kernel is asked to abort each (CancelIoEx on that
// operation alone), they stay listed, and each abort's completion comes to the worker port and
// answers it ('udpCompleted'). 'n' is how many were asked. A handle that names nothing: null
fn askWaiting(sh: &Shared, h: Handle, write: bool, n: &mut usize) *OpCore {
  if !sh.lock(h) {return null;}
  let e = sh.entry(h.index);
  var head = if write {(*e).swait} else {(*e).rwait};
  #if #os == .windows {
    // Counted whether or not anything is listed: a datagram started under the shared lock and not yet
    // listed finds the count changed when 'listStarted' lists it, and asks the kernel to abort it
    if write {(*e).wcancels = (*e).wcancels + 1u32;} else {(*e).rcancels = (*e).rcancels + 1u32;}
    var q = head;
    while q as usize != 0usize {
      let r = q as &mut OpCore;
      if r.asked == askedNone {
        r.asked = askedCancel;
        CancelIoEx((*e).raw, q as *u8);
        *n = *n + 1usize;
      }
      q = (*q).hnext;
    }
    head = null;
  } #else {
    if write {(*e).swait = null;} else {(*e).rwait = null;}
    var q = head;
    while q as usize != 0usize {
      (*q).side = onNone;
      (*q).hprev = null;
      *n = *n + 1usize;
      q = (*q).hnext;
    }
  }
  sh.unlock(h);
  head
}

// A new table, its directory empty, the Loop its one holder; none if there is
// no memory for it (then nothing is issued off the Loop's thread)
fn makeShared() Shared {
  let s = libc.calloc(1usize, mem.sizeof[Slab]()) as *Slab;
  let d = libc.calloc(usize.from(shChunks), mem.sizeof[usize]()) as *usize;
  let c = libc.calloc(usize.from(stripes * 8u32), mem.sizeof[u64]()) as *u64;
  if s as usize == 0usize || d as usize == 0usize || c as usize == 0usize {
    libc.free(s as *u8);
    libc.free(d as *u8);
    libc.free(c as *u8);
    return Shared.none();
  }
  (*s).dir = d;
  (*s).cont = c;
  (*s).holders = 1u64;
  sharedLive.add(1u32);
  Shared(s as usize)
}

// Cell i published for generation gen, on the Loop's thread (the only writer of
// the directory and of an entry's fields): its fields first, then its
// generation, released. A cell no chunk could be made for stays unpublished,
// and its operations go the Loop's way
fn publishEntry(sh: &Shared, i: u32, gen: u32, raw: usize, kind: u32, skip: u32, worker: u32) {
  let ci = i / shChunk;
  if ci >= shChunks {return;}
  let slot = (*(sh.at as *Slab)).dir + usize.from(ci);
  var c = mem.atomicLoad[usize](slot, MemOrder.Relaxed);
  if c == 0usize {
    c = libc.calloc(usize.from(shChunk), mem.sizeof[SEntry]()) as usize;
    if c == 0usize {return;}
    mem.atomicStore[usize](slot, c, MemOrder.Release);
  }
  let e = (c as *SEntry) + usize.from(i % shChunk);
  (*e).raw = raw;
  (*e).kind = kind;
  (*e).skip = skip;
  (*e).worker = worker;
  (*e).hold = 0u32;
  (*e).rwait = null;
  (*e).swait = null;
  (*e).closing = 0u32;
  mem.atomicStore[u32](&(*e).gen as *u32, gen, MemOrder.Release);
}

// The Loop's own port, kept in the table for the workers that forward a connect's completion to it
fn setLoopPort(sh: &Shared, port: usize) {
  if sh.at != 0usize {(*(sh.at as *Slab)).loopPort = port;}
}

// A worker-side socket's entry is one: set by the Loop's thread, on the entry
// of cell i, before any other thread knows the handle
fn markWorkerEntry(sh: &Shared, i: u32) {
  let e = sh.entry(i);
  if e as usize == 0usize {return;}
  (*e).worker = 1u32;
}

// Cell i unpublished, under its lock: a thread issuing on it has finished
// (and queued what follows) before this returns, and none starts after. The
// records waiting worker-side on it (UDP ON THE WORKER PORT) leave its lists
// with it: the first of them, chained on 'hnext' (receives, then sends), is
// answered, or let go, by the caller; null when none waits
fn unpublishEntry(sh: &Shared, i: u32) *OpCore {
  let e = sh.entry(i);
  if e as usize == 0usize {return null;}
  if mem.atomicLoad[u32](&(*e).gen as *u32, MemOrder.Relaxed) == 0u32 {return null;}
  sh.acquire(e);
  mem.atomicStore[u32](&(*e).gen as *u32, 0u32, MemOrder.Release);
  var head = (*e).rwait;
  var last = head;
  if last as usize != 0usize {
    while (*last).hnext as usize != 0usize {last = (*last).hnext;}
    (*last).hnext = (*e).swait;
  } else {
    head = (*e).swait;
  }
  (*e).rwait = null;
  (*e).swait = null;
  (*e).worker = 0u32;
  var q = head;
  while q as usize != 0usize {
    (*q).side = onNone;
    (*q).hprev = null;
    q = (*q).hnext;
  }
  sh.release(e);
  head
}

// Record p in flight from now, stamped as the Loop's 'stamp' does, but not
// counted: the Loop counts it if it is handed over (linkIssued)
fn stampIssued(p: *OpCore, now: Instant) {
  let r = p as &mut OpCore;
  setState(p, stFlight);
  r.asked = askedNone;
  r.hit = false;
  r.lapses = false;
  r.early = false;
  r.late = false;
  r.submitted = now;
  r.dequeued = now;
  r.newSock = invalidHandle;
  r.newHandle = Handle.none();
  r.timer = TimerId.none();
  r.jfd = 0i32 - 1i32;
  r.status = 0u32;
  r.n = 0usize;
  r.code = 0u32;
  r.done = 0usize;
  r.ustate = usNone;
  r.ov = Overlapped(0usize, 0usize, 0u32, 0u32, 0usize);
}

// Record p answered where it was issued: its outcome in it, let go
fn answeredHere(p: *OpCore, status: u32, n: usize, code: u32) u32 {
  let r = p as &mut OpCore;
  r.link = linkNone;
  r.status = status;
  r.n = n;
  r.code = code;
  setState(p, stDone);
  issueDone
}

// Windows: the receive or send on raw, whose completions skip the port on
// success. Pending: the record is the kernel's, marked in transit BEFORE the
// call, and nothing of it is touched here after. 'started': the entry is held SHARED, not exclusive: the
// record is marked usStarted before the call, and a pending one answers issueStarted, for 'listStarted'
// (STARTED UNDER THE SHARED LOCK); a call that answers at once, or fails, leaves it usNone
#if #os == .windows {
fn winIssue(p: *OpCore, raw: usize, now: Instant, worker: bool, started: bool) u32 {
  let r = p as &mut OpCore;
  if r.kind == diag.kindSend && r.isGather() {return winIssueGather(p, raw, now, worker);}
  var data = null as *u8;
  var len = 0usize;
  if r.kind == diag.kindSend {
    data, len = sendData(r);
    if len > 0xFFFFFFFFusize {return issueNot;}
  } else if r.kind == diag.kindSendTo {
    if r.bufLen() > 0xFFFFFFFFusize {return issueNot;}
  }
  stampIssued(p, now);
  r.raw = raw;
  // A worker-side socket's completion comes to the worker port, whose waiter
  // answers it; the Loop never holds the record, so it is not in transit
  r.link = if worker {linkNone} else {linkTransit};
  // Before the kernel call: its completion, taken by any thread, then finds this store behind it
  if started {mem.atomicStore[u32](&r.ustate as *u32, usStarted, MemOrder.Release);}
  var got = 0u32;
  var rc = 0i32;
  let from = r.sockAddr();
  if r.kind == diag.kindRecvFrom || r.kind == diag.kindRecvMany {
    // One datagram, the sender into the record's address area (a RecvMany's
    // is Windows' batch of one, into its first buffer)
    let room = r.bufRoom();
    r.wsa.len = if room > 0xFFFFFFFFusize {0xFFFFFFFFu32} else {u32.from(room)};
    r.wsa.buf = r.bufBase() + r.bufLen();
    r.flags = 0u32;
    (*from).size = 28i32;
    rc = WSARecvFrom(raw, &mut r.wsa as *WsaBuf, 1u32, &mut got as *u32, &mut r.flags, from as *u8,
        &mut (*from).size, p as *u8, null);
  } else if r.kind == diag.kindSendTo {
    r.wsa.len = u32.from(r.bufLen());
    r.wsa.buf = r.bufBase();
    rc = WSASendTo(raw, &mut r.wsa as *WsaBuf, 1u32, &mut got as *u32, 0u32, from as *u8, (*from).size,
        p as *u8, null);
  } else if r.kind == diag.kindRecvReady {
    // A zero-byte receive, holding no buffer: it ends when bytes are there, or the stream has ended
    r.wsa.len = 0u32;
    r.wsa.buf = null;
    r.flags = 0u32;
    rc = WSARecv(raw, &mut r.wsa as *WsaBuf, 1u32, &mut got as *u32, &mut r.flags, p as *u8, null);
  } else if r.kind == diag.kindRecv {
    let room = r.bufRoom();
    r.wsa.len = if room > 0xFFFFFFFFusize {0xFFFFFFFFu32} else {u32.from(room)};
    r.wsa.buf = r.bufBase() + r.bufLen();
    r.flags = 0u32;
    rc = WSARecv(raw, &mut r.wsa as *WsaBuf, 1u32, &mut got as *u32, &mut r.flags, p as *u8, null);
  } else {
    r.wsa.len = u32.from(len);
    r.wsa.buf = data;
    rc = WSASend(raw, &mut r.wsa as *WsaBuf, 1u32, &mut got as *u32, 0u32, p as *u8, null);
  }
  if rc == 0i32 {
    // Done at once, and no packet will come: the port is skipped on success
    if started {mem.atomicStore[u32](&r.ustate as *u32, usNone, MemOrder.Relaxed);}
    r.dequeued = Instant.now();
    if r.kind == diag.kindRecv || r.kind == diag.kindRecvFrom || r.kind == diag.kindRecvMany {r.bufGrow(usize.from(got));}
    if r.kind == diag.kindRecvMany {
      r.batchOne(false);
      return answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeDone, 1usize, 0u32);
    }
    return answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeDone, usize.from(got), 0u32);
  }
  let code = wsaError();
  // Pending: nothing of the record is touched here after (a completion may be taken and, if it wins the word,
  // left for the issuer to answer: listStarted)
  if code == errorIoPending {return if started {issueStarted} else {issuePending};}
  // Refused at once: no packet either, the same answer the Loop's start gives
  if started {mem.atomicStore[u32](&r.ustate as *u32, usNone, MemOrder.Relaxed);}
  if code == errorMsgSize && r.kind == diag.kindRecvMany {
    r.batchCutAtOnce();
    return answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeDone, 1usize, 0u32);
  }
  answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeFailed, 0usize, code)
}
}

// Windows: a gather, one WSASend over a WSABUF for each segment of the chain
// (the Loop's startGather). Winsock captures the array when the overlapped
// call is made, so it lives only for the call: on the stack up to 16 segments,
// else in a block freed after it. A gather over 0xFFFFFFFF bytes, or one the
// block cannot be had for, is issueNot
#if #os == .windows {
fn winIssueGather(p: *OpCore, raw: usize, now: Instant, worker: bool) u32 {
  let r = p as &mut OpCore;
  if r.gatherLen() > 0xFFFFFFFFusize {return issueNot;}
  let segs = r.gatherSegments();
  var spansHere = Array[Span, 16]() <- fill Span(null, 0usize);
  var wsaHere = Array[WsaBuf, 16]() <- fill WsaBuf(0u32, null);
  var spans = &mut spansHere as *Span;
  var bufs = &mut wsaHere as *WsaBuf;
  let big = segs > 16usize;
  if big {
    spans = libc.malloc(segs * mem.sizeof[Span]()) as *Span;
    bufs = libc.malloc(segs * mem.sizeof[WsaBuf]()) as *WsaBuf;
    if spans as usize == 0usize || bufs as usize == 0usize {
      libc.free(spans as *u8);
      libc.free(bufs as *u8);
      return issueNot;
    }
  }
  let n = r.gatherSpans(0usize, spans, segs);
  var k = 0usize;
  while k < n {
    (*(bufs + k)).len = u32.from((*(spans + k)).len);
    (*(bufs + k)).buf = (*(spans + k)).base;
    k = k + 1usize;
  }
  stampIssued(p, now);
  r.raw = raw;
  r.link = if worker {linkNone} else {linkTransit};
  var got = 0u32;
  let rc = WSASend(raw, bufs, u32.from(n), &mut got as *u32, 0u32, p as *u8, null);
  let code = if rc == 0i32 {0u32} else {wsaError()};
  if big {
    libc.free(spans as *u8);
    libc.free(bufs as *u8);
  }
  if rc == 0i32 {
    r.dequeued = Instant.now();
    return answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeDone, usize.from(got), 0u32);
  }
  if code == errorIoPending {return issuePending;}
  answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeFailed, 0usize, code)
}
}

// Linux: the receive or send on the non-blocking descriptor raw
#if #os != .windows {
fn lxIssue(p: *OpCore, raw: usize, now: Instant, worker: bool) u32 {
  let r = p as &mut OpCore;
  let fd = fdOf(raw);
  // A send of a worker-side stream goes on from the byte 'done' reached, once it is in flight (stamped):
  // a try after a partial send, or the edge's retry (TCP ON THE WORKER PORT). The first try starts at 0
  let resumed = r.state == stFlight;
  if r.kind == diag.kindRecvReady {
    // Data to read, or the end of the stream, without taking either
    var probe = 0u8;
    var n = sysRecv(fd, &mut probe as *u8, 1usize, msgPeek);
    while n < 0i64 && lastErrno() == eintr {n = sysRecv(fd, &mut probe as *u8, 1usize, msgPeek);}
    if n < 0i64 && lastErrno() == eagain {return issueNot;}
    stampIssued(p, now);
    if n >= 0i64 {return answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeDone, 0usize, 0u32);}
    return answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeFailed, 0usize, u32.from(lastErrno()));
  }
  if r.kind == diag.kindRecv {
    var n = sysRecv(fd, r.bufBase() + r.bufLen(), r.bufRoom(), 0i32);
    while n < 0i64 && lastErrno() == eintr {n = sysRecv(fd, r.bufBase() + r.bufLen(), r.bufRoom(), 0i32);}
    if n < 0i64 && lastErrno() == eagain {return issueNot;}
    stampIssued(p, now);
    if n >= 0i64 {
      r.bufGrow(usize.from(n));
      return answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeDone, usize.from(n), 0u32);
    }
    return answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeFailed, 0usize, u32.from(lastErrno()));
  }
  if r.kind == diag.kindRecvFrom {
    // One datagram. MSG_TRUNC makes the answer its whole length, so a cut one
    // is told from one that fitted exactly
    let room = r.bufRoom();
    let from = r.sockAddr();
    (*from).size = 28i32;
    var n = sysRecvfrom(fd, r.bufBase() + r.bufLen(), room, msgTrunc, from as *u8, &mut (*from).size as *u32);
    while n < 0i64 && lastErrno() == eintr {
      (*from).size = 28i32;
      n = sysRecvfrom(fd, r.bufBase() + r.bufLen(), room, msgTrunc, from as *u8, &mut (*from).size as *u32);
    }
    if n < 0i64 && lastErrno() == eagain {return issueNot;}
    stampIssued(p, now);
    if n < 0i64 {return answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeFailed, 0usize, u32.from(lastErrno()));}
    if usize.from(n) > room {
      r.bufGrow(room);
      return answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeFailed, 0usize, datagramCut());
    }
    r.bufGrow(usize.from(n));
    return answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeDone, usize.from(n), 0u32);
  }
  if r.kind == diag.kindRecvMany {
    // Every datagram waiting, up to the buffers given (the Loop's lxTry)
    let n = lxRecvMany(fd, r);
    if n < 0i64 && lastErrno() == eagain {return issueNot;}
    stampIssued(p, now);
    if n >= 1i64 {return answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeDone, usize.from(n), 0u32);}
    if n == 0i64 {return answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeFailed, 0usize, noRoom());}
    return answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeFailed, 0usize, u32.from(lastErrno()));
  }
  if r.kind == diag.kindSendTo {
    // One datagram, whole or not at all
    let to = r.sockAddr();
    let len = r.bufLen();
    var n = sysSendto(fd, r.bufBase(), len, msgNoSignal, to as *u8, u32.from((*to).size));
    while n < 0i64 && lastErrno() == eintr {
      n = sysSendto(fd, r.bufBase(), len, msgNoSignal, to as *u8, u32.from((*to).size));
    }
    if n < 0i64 && lastErrno() == eagain {return issueNot;}
    stampIssued(p, now);
    if n < 0i64 {return answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeFailed, 0usize, u32.from(lastErrno()));}
    return answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeDone, usize.from(n), 0u32);
  }
  if r.isGather() {
    // A gather: sendmsg over the segments until the socket has taken them all
    // or would block (the Loop's lxTry). Nothing sent and would block: not
    // issued. Part sent: 'done' keeps it, and the Loop goes on from there
    let glen = r.gatherLen();
    var spans = Array[Span, 64]() <- fill Span(null, 0usize);
    let from0 = if resumed {r.done} else {0usize};
    var sentg = from0;
    var failedg = 0i32;
    while sentg < glen && failedg == 0i32 {
      let cnt = r.gatherSpans(sentg, &mut spans as *Span, gatherBatch);
      var msg = MsgHdrLx(null, 0u32, 0u32, &mut spans as *Span, cnt, null, 0usize, 0i32, 0i32);
      let n = sysSendmsg(fd, &mut msg as *MsgHdrLx, msgNoSignal);
      if n >= 0i64 {
        sentg = sentg + usize.from(n);
      } else {
        let e = lastErrno();
        if e != eintr {failedg = e;}
      }
    }
    if failedg == eagain && sentg == from0 {return issueNot;}
    stampIssued(p, now);
    r.done = sentg;
    if failedg == eagain {
      r.link = if worker {linkNone} else {linkTransit};
      return issuePending;
    }
    if failedg != 0i32 {
      return answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeFailed, 0usize, u32.from(failedg));
    }
    return answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeDone, glen, 0u32);
  }
  var data = null as *u8;
  var len = 0usize;
  data, len = sendData(r);
  let from = if resumed {r.done} else {0usize};
  var sent = from;
  var failed = 0i32;
  while sent < len && failed == 0i32 {
    let n = sysSend(fd, data + sent, len - sent, msgNoSignal);
    if n >= 0i64 {
      sent = sent + usize.from(n);
    } else {
      let e = lastErrno();
      if e != eintr {failed = e;}
    }
  }
  if failed == eagain && sent == from {return issueNot;}
  stampIssued(p, now);
  r.done = sent;
  if failed == eagain {
    // Part of it sent: the rest goes on from 'done' on the Loop, never again
    // from the start (linkIssued keeps it); a worker-side stream's, here (issueWaiting lists it)
    r.link = if worker {linkNone} else {linkTransit};
    return issuePending;
  }
  if failed != 0i32 {
    return answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeFailed, 0usize, u32.from(failed));
  }
  answeredHere(p, diag.outcomeDone, len, 0u32)
}
}
